# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
sidgwick-methods-2.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2 and sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2.3 have "indulge_painful"; sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2.2 has "indulge_painful" and "indulge_painful → not_coincident"; they differ when only "there are numerous examples of men who continue to feel and indulge desires whose gratification they know by ample experience to be attended with more pain than pleasure" is true, which the first allows
sidgwick-methods-2.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "there are numerous examples of men who continue to feel and indulge desires whose gratification they know by ample experience to be attended with more pain than pleasure" is true and "objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure are not exactly coincident" is false. Is S5, "Now we have seen (Book i. chap. iv.) that these two are not exactly coincident--indeed we find numerous examples of men who continue not only to feel but to indulge desires, the gratification of which they know by ample experience to be attended with more pain than pleasure.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-2.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2 and sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2.3 have "think_desirable"; sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2.2 has "think_desirable" and "think_desirable → compromise" and "no_distinguish ∧ not_coincident ∧ compromise → ¬expresses_experience"; they differ when only "the common estimate of different sources of happiness does not distinguish between objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure", "objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure are not exactly coincident", "men are apt to think desirable what they strongly desire, whether or not they have found it conducive to happiness on the whole", "the common opinion tends to represent a compromise between the average force of desires and the average experience of the consequences of gratifying them" and "the current estimate of the desirability of objects of pursuit can be taken to express simply men's experience of pleasure and pain" are true, which the first allows
sidgwick-methods-2.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "the common estimate of different sources of happiness does not distinguish between objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure", "objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure are not exactly coincident", "men are apt to think desirable what they strongly desire, whether or not they have found it conducive to happiness on the whole", "the common opinion tends to represent a compromise between the average force of desires and the average experience of the consequences of gratifying them" and "the current estimate of the desirability of objects of pursuit can be taken to express simply men's experience of pleasure and pain" are true. Is S6, "And therefore the current estimate of the desirability of objects of pursuit cannot be taken to express simply men’s experience of pleasure and pain: for men are apt to think desirable what they strongly desire, whether or not they have found it conducive to happiness on the whole: and so the common opinion will tend to represent a compromise between the average force of desires and the average experience of the consequences of gratifying them.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2 and sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2.3 have "indulge_painful → not_coincident" and "think_desirable → compromise" and "no_distinguish ∧ not_coincident ∧ compromise → ¬expresses_experience"; sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the common estimate of different sources of happiness does not distinguish between objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure", "objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure are not exactly coincident", "there are numerous examples of men who continue to feel and indulge desires whose gratification they know by ample experience to be attended with more pain than pleasure", "men are apt to think desirable what they strongly desire, whether or not they have found it conducive to happiness on the whole", "the common opinion tends to represent a compromise between the average force of desires and the average experience of the consequences of gratifying them" and "the current estimate of the desirability of objects of pursuit can be taken to express simply men's experience of pleasure and pain" are true, which the second allows
